      "textEn": "401\n\nHe said, \"You have given a promise of three steps, but in just two steps, your entirety will be finished.\"\n\"It will be destroyed.\" Then how will you be able to give them a place for the third step?\" Shukracharya did not know\nhow God protects his devotee. A devotee should, in the service of God,\nput all his rights at stake, but he is always protected, and he never\nis defeated. Shukracharya, from a material point of view, guessed and thought that Bali Maharaj, in any way, could not fulfill his promise to the celibate Lord Vamanadeva. Those who are steadfast in their devotion, I consider them to be the ones who are not given to hell, it is heard.\nThe one who is spoken of, the one who is not spoken of, you are the one who is spoken of. 35. Meaning of the word\n\nNishtam—Eternal abode; Te—Yours; Narake—In hell; Manye—I think; Hi—Indeed; Apradatuh—Not fulfilling the promise;\nOf a person who is about to fulfill; Pratishrutam—To whom a promise has been given; Pratishrutasya—Of a promise given by someone; Yah—He;\nAneeshah—Incompetent; Pratipadayitum—In fulfilling completely; Bhavan—You. You will definitely be unable to fulfill your promise, and I think that because of your\ninability, you will have to eternally reside in hell. Those who are praised by that, by whom the profession is fulfilled. Donation, sacrifice, penance, and actions are the state of being engaged in the world. 36. Meaning of the word\n\nNa—No; Tat—That; Danam—of charity; Prashansanti—Saints praise; Yena—By which; Vrittiha—Profession; Vipadyate—Falls into trouble; Danam—Charity; Yagya—Yagya; Tapah—Tapasya; Karma—Desireful action; Loke—In this world; Vrittimatah—According to one's profession; Yatah—As it is.\nA wise person does not praise a donation that puts one's own livelihood in danger. Only one who is fully capable of earning their livelihood can perform charity, sacrifice, penance, and selfless actions.\n(These actions are impossible for someone who cannot support themselves). For dharma, for fame, for desire, for one's own people. The fivefold division is here, and also the joy of non-division. 37. Lexicon"
