      "textEn": "402\n\nFor dharma—for righteousness; for fame—for one's own fame; for wealth—to increase one's own opulence; for desire—for the satisfaction of desires; for self—for one's own; for progeny—for the nurturing of offspring; for fivefold—for these five different goals; by dividing—by dividing;\nBy wealth—by one's accumulated wealth; here—in this world; beyond—in the next world; and—and; delight—enjoys. Therefore, the wise one should divide his accumulated wealth into five parts—\nFor religion, for fame, for opulence, for sensual gratification, and for the sustenance of family members. Such a person remains happy in this world and also in the afterlife. Meaning: The scriptures command that if someone has wealth, it should be divided into five parts and given—one part for religion, the second part for fame, the third for opulence, the fourth\nfor sensual gratification, and the fifth part for the maintenance of family members. But in the present time,\nall people, due to being devoid of knowledge, spend all their wealth for the satisfaction of their family.\nSrila\nRoop Goswami gave us education from his own example that fifty percent of accumulated wealth should be for Krishna,\ntwenty-five percent on oneself and the remaining twenty-five percent should be spent on one's family members. The main objective of a human being should be to progress in Krishna consciousness. In this, dharma, artha, and kama\nwill be included. But, because his family members also have some expectations of benefit, therefore, one should satisfy them by giving some portion of their accumulated wealth. It is a classical order. Even now, listen to the many-hearted, the best of demons.\nThat which is spoken of as truth, is false, that is also false. 3. Meaning of the word\n\nEven in this context—what is the truth, what is not; Bahu-richaih—those which are called Bahvacha Shruti through Vedic mantras and are the evidence of the Vedas; Geetam—sung; Shrunu—listen; Me—from me; Asur-satam