      "textEn": "403\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nTruth—the real truth; flower-fruit—flower and fruit; to understand—should be understood; of the self-tree—of the body-like tree;\nAs it is sung—as it is described in the Vedas; if the tree does not live—if the tree itself does not remain alive; that—that (flower-fruit); not—not; if—if;\nFalsehood—lie; root—root; of the self—of the body. It is the order of the Vedas that the real result of the body-like tree is the best fruits and flowers that come from it. But if this body-like tree does not remain, then there is no possibility of these real fruits and flowers\nhaving any existence. Even if the body is based on falsehood,\neven then, without the body-like tree, there can be no real fruits and flowers. Meaning: This verse explains that in this body, without even a bit of untruth, the real truth cannot exist. The Mayavadis say—Brahma is truth, the world is illusion—the soul is truth and illusion. But Vaishnava philosophers do not accept Mayaavada philosophy.\nIf, for the sake of argument, this physical world is considered false, then the soul trapped in Maya cannot get out of it without the help of the body. Without the help of the body, no one can follow religion, nor can anyone\nthink of philosophical perfection. Therefore, flowers and fruits (pushpaphalam) are to be obtained as a result of the body. Without the help of the body, that fruit cannot even be obtained. Therefore, Vaishnava philosophy advocates detachment. It is not that all attention should be given to the body,\nbut along with that, neglect of the body's nourishment should also not be done. Until\nthe body remains, a human being, by fully studying the Vedic commands, can attain perfection at the end of life.\nIt is possible. It is explained in the Bhagavad Gita (.6)— yam yam api smaran bhavam tyajatyante kalevaram. At the time of death, every single thing is tested. Therefore, although this body is perishable, not eternal, a human\ncan take the best possible service from it and make his life complete.\nYatha vrikshaanmoolah shushyatyudvartatechirat. And destroyed, not destroyed, the soul is immediately absorbed, there is no doubt. 40. Meaning of the word\n\nTat—therefore; Yatha—as; Vrikshah—tree; Unmoolah—uprooted; Shushyati—dries up; Udvartate—falls; Achirat—quickly; Evam—like that; Nashtah—destroyed; Anritah—this perishable body; Sadyah—immediately; Atma—body;\nShushyet—dries up; Na—not; Sanshayah—no doubt; Kosha—body;\nShushyet—dries up; Na—not; Sanshayah—no doubt;\nSanshayah—no doubt; Kosha—body;\nSanshayah—no doubt; Atma—body;\nShushyet—dries up; Na—not; Sanshayah—no doubt;\nKosha—body;\nShushyet—dries up; Na—not; Sanshayah—no doubt;\nKosha—body;\nShushyet—dries up; Na—not; S"
